A||||20||GarethWright||Loud Ticking Now Louder, Injectors(?)||||1||
Z||000000||GarethWright||05-30-2001||02:06 AM||||I have always heard a soft ticking noise from my engine while I drive, more noticeable with sunroof open or windows down, and I have been told it was the fuel injectors.  On the highway the other day, normal driving, the ticking became Louder, like Noticeably louder.  Is this likely to be caused by the aging injectors, or is it more likely to be the dreaded exhaust manifold crack I have heard about?<BR>Thanks<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
Z||000001||tgowens||05-30-2001||02:26 AM||||more than likely it is the dreaded manifold crack..........<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero/index.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>                <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero/3GTS_SIG.gif">                 </A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cliff D.||05-30-2001||02:34 AM||||When I had my exhaust manifold crack, I didn't hear ticking, It was more like the sound of an old volkswagon. When I got the new manifolds on I did hear ticking from the injector area. My fuel pressure diagnosis led me to "find the leaking injector and fix it" says the holy shop book. I just finished removing and cleaning everything from the intake manifold up including the injectors (of which 3 had terrible cracks on that little hat looking plastic piece on the spray end and whent to the dump). I probably won't get it back together in time to tell you what effect the replacement injectors have on the ticking noise. Maybe we are in the footsteps of someone who knows. I am frightened though where the missing 1/4" piece of one of the above mentioned "hats" could be.||||reg||1
Z||000003||GarethWright||05-30-2001||03:35 PM||||Oops, forgot to mention, the ticking happens when I'm on the gas, when I'm coasting with my foot off the gas pedal, the ticking goes away.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||05-30-2001||03:44 PM||||Sounds alot like injectors, my car is the same way.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fiero_24||05-30-2001||04:00 PM||||Bad news, but I just sold an 85 SE that came down with the problem 7 days after selling it.  It turns out that the rod bearing went out, and kept getting worse.  I have driven on 2 bad exhaust manifolds and never heard a ticking.||||reg||8
Z||000006||Fiero_24||05-30-2001||04:02 PM||||It could also be a valve pushrod lose, or a bad valve.  It could be an injector but I have never had an injector go bad, just rod bearings, so I have never heard a bad injector.  It started out as a clicking and turned into a knocking sound after a while.||||reg||1
Z||000007||FLASHY||05-30-2001||04:53 PM||||Oh great, I have that ticking noise too. So I guess it's not going to be a $4.99 repair job huh?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fiero_24||05-30-2001||05:43 PM||||It may not be a bearing, but in my case it was.  I always check valves first because I have heard cars low on oil and heard the valves chattering.  If you hear ticking and it is only getting worse it is a bearing.  The car I sold started with a light tick that got louder and louder, and now is a knock.  It could be a valve slapping against the piston head too.  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||87GTZ34||05-30-2001||06:01 PM||||Ticking while on the gas is 'usually' a cracked manifold.  When a car is under load the pressure in the exhaust rises and in this case is hissing out of the crack.  I had mine welded a couple of months ago and now just the normal injector noise is present.  I'd put my money on the front manifold... GL||||reg||1
Z||000010||DaveL||05-30-2001||06:41 PM||||My 87 SE 2.5 had a nasty tick when I bought it about a year ago. Had it in the shop for what turned out to be a bad ECM, and the mech tried to change the O2 sensor. It twisted out of the manifold allright, but all the threads came with it. It had a rust hole right on the top of the tubes for the middle 2 cylinders as well. Finally ended up putting a new manifold on it. The most dramatic difference was the sound. Almost no tick at all. <P>Well here I am a year later, and it has returned with a vengence. Sounds like a metal on metal rapping that appears and disappears mysteriously. Runs very strong, idles as smooth as a baby's butt, but noisy. Gets a lot quieter when it heats up. No tick at idle or full throttle. Arrgh I really need to take of the whole air cleaner so I can get to the left side with the hose. I hope I do find an exhaust leak.<P>Changed the oil and added a quart if Rislone and it changed a little, got quieter. I've also read a few posts about the GM replacement PROM chips for a problem like this. <P>------------------<BR>||||reg||11
Z||000011||grinthock||05-30-2001||06:45 PM||||Just gonna post my 2 cents, it could be almost anything you say TICK, I say TOCK, Or Ping, Or Knock... It's really hard to tell, but it could be a lifter, rod, manifold crack hell could even be a worn bearing or something, it's just too hard to tell...<P>To TRY to find where the noise is coming from take a peice of solid wood and put it against your ear, now touch it to parts of your rocker covers and listen, it's a trick that lets you hear inside, MIGHT give you a better idea.||||reg||1
Z||000012||sjp777||05-30-2001||08:31 PM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 87GTZ34:<BR><B>Ticking while on the gas is 'usually' a cracked manifold.  When a car is under load the pressure in the exhaust rises and in this case is hissing out of the crack.  I had mine welded a couple of months ago and now just the normal injector noise is present.  I'd put my money on the front manifold... GL</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Bingo, we have a winner.<P>If it sounds like a briggs and straton when you get on the gas, it is almost certainly the manifold, and most likely the front one.<P>It's really hard to see back there. A good trick to check is to take an old garden hose and cut about 2' off one end. With the car running, feed the end with the metal connector behind the engine until that end contacts the manifold. Put your ear to the other end and listen as you moved the metal end along the manifold. when it gets to the cracked area, you will definately notice a change in the sound, even with the car at idle. My crack was so big I actually got some exhaust gases flowing back up the hose.<P>Obviously the purpose of leaving the metal end on the hose that touches the manifold is to prevent melting the hose all over it.<P><BR>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_2.jpg">      <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A><P><p>[This message has been edited by sjp777 (edited 05-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||87GTZ34||05-30-2001||09:56 PM||||Gareth, the theme of this former post should be the first thought that runs thru your mind when troubleshooting any new problem with our beloved Fieros...  It has always been a mainstay of my troubleshooting routine when I was a proffesional mechanic.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008569.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/008569.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||pontiacs4me||05-31-2001||02:54 AM||||Up until last week my car sounded like a briggs and stratton.  When I first bought the engine it had a slight tick that wasnt really noticable unless you rolled the windows up and turned off the radio, and got on the gas real good, and listened real hard, so I figured it was just an injector ticking.  So I put some injector cleaner in it and went on my merry way.  Well since feb. 22nd I have put 7 thousand miles on my car, and it had got real loud, progressively got worse on the power tour, to the point that I couldnt sleep at night because all I could hear at night was the relentless ticking of my exhaust leak.  It was leaking from the front manifold, from the port closest to the driver side.  There was carbon burn on the metal shield.  So finally I got a day off work and decided that was the day to fix the leak.  I dropped the front of the cradle to get some clearance, and I pulled the manifold.  And what a suprise 2 bolts were barely more than finger tight.  Why I wonder, maybe because their STRIPPED!  so I went to a hardware store and found a bolt that was slightly longer, and grade 8, and a different thread, And I drove it in there as hard as I could.  After I installed a new gasket of course.  So then I get home and post a message about the experience of the day, and someone mentions that that shield was recalled.  I figure oh well my exhaust leak is fixed, so who cares.  And the next morning I fire up my car, to go pick up my dad, and as I walk away to go back inside, what do I hear through that vent, tick, tick, oh so softly.  Damn I say, I pop the deck, and get a closer listen.  tick tick tick.  Oh well, its barely noticable, just like when I bought the engine.  So I figure another 7k and Ill be doing it again.  This time the sheild is going bye bye.  Boy I sure hope those bolts come out.||||reg||1
Z||000015||GarethWright||05-31-2001||04:45 PM||||Hello all again, thanks for all the advice, I tried Grinthocks listening through the pole trick, as I don't expect Dad would appreciate me cutting up his hoses  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I can hear loud ticking at the passenger side end of the fuel rail, and when I put the pole on the rear(trunk-side) manifold bolts.  I couldn't get to all the front manifold before the end of my stick started smoking  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I figured that I would let it cool for a while and try again.  But when I set the pole against the downpipe on the front manifold, all I could hear was the exhaust going through.  Where else should I listen?  The ticking isnt getting any worse, just louder at higher RPMs.  Unfortuneatly, we don't have a Briggs and Straton, so I can't compare to that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Thanks again for all the help,<BR>Gareth||||reg||1
Z||000016||phreakboy4||05-31-2001||08:36 PM||||My girlfriends car had a ticking during accelerating and I found that it was a leak at the intake manifold. so that should be another place to check out.<P>84 2M4 coupe                  Sean V||||reg||1
Z||000017||GarethWright||06-01-2001||03:17 PM||||Where on the intake manifold? I cleaned all mine out a few weeks ago, but I put everything back to proper torque settings, and checked them again after a week or so.<P>Also, it only ticks in Drive Gears, not in Neutral or Park, but I figure thats because in neutral and park, the engine isnt having any load put on it such as getting the car to move<p>[This message has been edited by GarethWright (edited 06-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||SLYGUY||10-07-2001||11:59 PM||||I hear a soft click when my 88GT is idling. I tried the broom stick thing and both manifolds sound okay. The click will fade away if you raise the throttle above 2K rpm.<P>?<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000019||GarethWright||10-08-2001||03:58 AM||||Dang, this one got pulled from the depths, so I guess its time for an update  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Nothing to report.<BR>In the interim we did get a new Lawnmower, yup Briggs & Straton, but it doesn's have a tick to it so I'm not sure where sjp777 was going with that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <BR>After several months of more study (and nothing being done about it) the ticking doesn't change with RPMs, so I am stumped, also I have a slight overheating problem now, so that gets priority over funny noises (which my car has plenty of, due to it having an interior with plastic bits).  Unless someone says "Hey, ticking causes overheating" or vice versa, and I can fix both at once, I will live with it a while longer.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000020||GarethWright||10-08-2001||03:59 AM||||Dang, this one got pulled from the depths, so I guess its time for an update  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Nothing to report.<BR>In the interim we did get a new Lawnmower, yup Briggs & Straton, but it doesn's have a tick to it so I'm not sure where sjp777 was going with that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <BR>After several months of more study (and nothing being done about it) the ticking doesn't change with RPMs, so I am stumped, also I have a slight overheating problem now, so that gets priority over funny noises (which my car has plenty of, due to it having an interior with plastic bits).  Unless someone says "Hey, ticking causes overheating" or vice versa, and I can fix both at once, I will live with it a while longer.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
